WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:08.000
Add 12.50 micromoles of nitrate-N to 0.50 litres of seawater containing 4.5 grams fresh mass of seaweed. This opening illustrates addition and mixing.

00:08.000 --> 00:14.000
After mixing, concentration is 25 micromoles per litre. Define this as experimental time zero. Track only the newly added nitrogen.

00:14.000 --> 00:30.000
The finite water reservoir loses nitrate while the tissue gains nitrogen. A no-seaweed control remains constant in this ideal example.

00:30.000 --> 00:46.000
The curve becomes shallower as concentration falls. Water nitrogen plus accumulated uptake still equals the amount added.

00:46.000 --> 01:06.000
Accumulated uptake is an amount. Its tangent gives the current whole-flask rate. A large accumulated amount can have a small current rate.

01:06.000 --> 01:28.000
For the first five minutes, multiply the concentration decrease by 0.50 litres, divide by 4.5 grams fresh mass, and divide by 5/60 hours. Mean uptake is 4.93 micromoles N per gram per hour.

01:28.000 --> 01:52.000
Pair concentration with uptake rate. Depletion carries time from right to left along the V–S curve. Five-minute interval estimates approximate the instantaneous model response.

01:52.000 --> 02:12.000
Multiple flasks start at different nutrient concentrations. Compare initial slopes with mass, volume and physiological condition matched. A depletion experiment instead follows successive intervals in one flask.

02:12.000 --> 02:24.000
Pause: restore nitrate to 25 micromoles per litre in the same flask. Predict the depletion slope, the uptake-curve position and the accumulated amount.

02:24.000 --> 02:46.000
Adding nitrate restores the initial model uptake rate of 5.00. The depletion curve becomes steep again. Previously acquired nitrogen stays in the tissue, and the total budget increases by the added pulse.

02:46.000 --> 03:02.000
Double fresh mass from 4.5 to 9 grams. Initial uptake per gram stays 5.00, while the whole-flask rate doubles. Both flasks begin with only 12.50 micromoles N available.

03:02.000 --> 03:16.000
Separate sampling example: removing 10 millilitres at concentration 10 exports 0.10 micromoles N but leaves the concentration unchanged. No uptake occurs in this example.

03:16.000 --> 03:24.000
Next, compare thin sheets, fine branches and thick thalli at matched tissue volume and physiology. How much surface is exposed by the same amount of tissue?
